# Lordle — Cursor Rules

## Project
Wordle clone with 10-minute timer, scoring system, and head-to-head match system.
Vite + React 19, Supabase (auth + stats), deployed to Vercel.

## Stack
- React 19, Vite, JavaScript (no TypeScript)
- Supabase: auth + database
- Vitest: testing
- Vercel: deployment

## Architecture — who owns what
- App.jsx: auth gate, screen routing, presentational Game wrapper. No game logic.
- useGameState.js: all game state, scoring, timer, keyboard. Single source of truth.
- useAuth.js: Supabase session only.
- gameLogic.js: pure functions only — evaluateGuess, computeGreenScore, computeBonus.
- matches.js: match service layer.
- words.js: single word list. ANSWERS and VALID_WORDS point to the same data.

## Conventions — never break these
- Inline styles only. No CSS modules, no CSS-in-JS libraries.
- React.memo on Tile and Key components. Do not remove.
- useMemo on letterStates and rows. Do not remove.
- Keyboard listener in useGameState uses useRef pattern — listener attaches once at mount, reads handleKeyRef.current. Never add handleKey to the listener effect's dep array.
- ANSWERS and VALID_WORDS are the same list. Do not split them.
- Word selection is random per game. No date-based selection.
- Animation timing is coupled between CSS and JS — tile-reveal is 0.7s, stagger is colIdx * 200ms, setRevealingRow fires at 1550ms, win/loss callback at 1600ms. If you change any of these, change all of them.

## Testing
- All logic changes require passing tests. Run: npm test
- Pre-commit hook (husky) runs vitest automatically — do not use --no-verify
- Tests live in src/test/

## Git
- Always commit to develop. Never directly to main.
